WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.590
- My mom's, like, incredible.

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:02.890
- She's very, very brave.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.990
I hope she knows that she's very brave.

00:00:05.311 --> 00:00:07.894
(bright music)

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:13.520
- The bravest thing that my mother ever did

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.120
was go to law school at 44 years old

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:19.170
after being out of the workforce for 20 some-odd years

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.990
and being a stay-at-home mom for just as long.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:22.540
She was an aspiring journalist,

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:23.750
and she ended up getting married

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:26.510
and having kids young, and then raised her kids.

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:28.430
She woke up one morning, as she proclaims,

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:29.640
and just felt like all of a sudden

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.830
she had no responsibilities, her children were grown,

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.820
and so she took the LSAT, passing

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:35.790
and going to law school and passing the bar

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.150
and is now a full-time lawyer.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.500
It's just taught me that the sky's the limit

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.020
and it's never too late if you want to

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:42.060
make a change in your life.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.870
My mom's, like, incredible.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:45.420
My dad died when I was younger,

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:47.020
and my mom just stepped up.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.440
It's hard to raise kids as a single parent.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.870
I have a disability, I have Tourette's Syndrome,

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:52.790
and it's harder when you're raising a kid with Tourette's.

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:55.080
I'm a comic, and my mother goes head to head with me.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.910
She's also the funniest person I've ever met.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.690
- My mom is a total queen.

00:00:58.870 --> 00:00:59.870
She's a judge.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.350
Just having that role model to look up to

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.220
throughout my life was really awesome.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.890
I would stop by her courtroom.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.900
One case, my mom addresses the defendant,

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:12.400
and she pulls her glasses down to the tip of her nose.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.310
"If you think that speaking to someone

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.413
in that manner is appropriate, you will end up

00:01:19.413 --> 00:01:21.150
right back in this courtroom,

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.840
and I will make sure it is in front of me."

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:24.913
And like...

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:27.080
Very queen.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.470
- My mom had my brother when she was 18

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:32.260
and had me at 21, so she was fairly young.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:33.930
We grew up on the border between

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.620
Tijuana and San Diego, living undocumented.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.620
My mom had been only in the U.S.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.940
for maybe four or five years, had been going

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:45.720
to English classes, and she came into my

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.720
second-grade classroom and surprised me by participating

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.600
in one of the monthly readings for their child.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.490
I was super-excited to see my mom there.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:56.750
Her voice was shaking.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.150
She had a very thick accent.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.480
I remember disregarding all of that

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.610
and being full of joy that my mom

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:04.220
was joining me in the classroom.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:07.420
I don't think I ever thanked my mom enough for that moment,

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.300
for all that vulnerability, for braving through

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.830
what might have been maybe an embarrassing moment.

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:18.660
- There is a specific moment that stands out for me.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.860
It was when I was in third grade.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.910
My dad, he got promoted.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.060
That entailed us having to move.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.390
It was the end of the world for me.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:27.870
I'm like, I have to leave my friends,

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.660
the Spice Girls album had just come out.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.410
So it was a huge transition.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.850
For my mom, at the time she was working

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:38.100
as a financial aid counselor at Federal State University.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:40.080
I knew how much she loved that job.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.890
Us moving to this new city also means

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.740
that you're having to really just change your whole life.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.240
So leaving my family, we're leaving friends.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.390
During that time of her trying to figure out

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:52.000
what am I going to do, applying for

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.900
job after job after job, I remember

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.310
some moments where it seemed like,

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.350
mom seems upset, but I remember her

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.300
just staying strong with her faith

00:02:59.570 --> 00:03:01.260
and always being very hopeful.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.360
After applying for a lot of positions

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.380
and going through applications and going to interviews,

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:09.060
she eventually got a position working

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.260
at the school that I attended.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.720
She just in that moment taught me a lot

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.110
about not ever doubting yourself.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:19.000
- I don't think she knows how brave she is.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.003
She...

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:22.680
Hoo, um...

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:25.260
Yeah, well, 'cause I'm...

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:26.450
I'm one of seven,

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:28.560
and, uh, geez, sorry.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:32.143
Ooh.

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:35.240
My dad passed away in 2006.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:37.970
The fact that she's carried on with

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.880
the amount of strength and care

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:46.430
and unconditional love and support for each of us

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:48.163
is huge.

00:03:48.163 --> 00:03:50.460
So I hope she knows that she's very brave.

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:52.230
She's very, very brave.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.180
- Personally and professionally, you can do it.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:56.430
If it's something that's going to

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:57.570
make you happy, to pursue it.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:58.820
I know people that have run into her

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:01.190
in court and say she's really damn good at her job.

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:04.680
- Putting me above herself and making sure that

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:08.880
I felt included in that parent family tradition

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.660
that was happening in the classroom.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:12.940
- The way that she can go from work

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:15.610
and would come home and cook dinner for us,

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.010
show up to all of my volleyball games,

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.830
I don't think that I always appreciated it,

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:22.920
but Mom, I love you so much.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:27.570
Thank you for being a total queen and a role model for me.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:30.140
- As long as you just trust the situation,

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:32.410
trust yourself, and hold onto your faith,

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:34.450
you can power through, really, anything.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:36.200
- I love you, Mom, happy Mother's Day.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.710
- Feliz dia de las madres, te amo, Mami.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:40.070
- Happy Mother's Day, Mom.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:42.430
I love you, and I know that you hate the beard,

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.520
but I will shave it off just for you.

